exports[`the bytes norn writes in a worktree a story unit, after a tick launched it and a brief rendered, then every other input kind: story brief 1`] = `
"# norn brief — lt-1 · build #1

You do one step of a larger delivery and report how it ended, once; code decides what happens next.

## Where you run

- You run unattended: no person reads this session. A question goes through \`norn ask\`, which parks the step until a person answers it.

## Story

\`lt-1\`: Parse and export (https://tracker.example/1)

## Your work

Build the thing.

## Inputs

Read these before you change anything:

- \`spec\` — the story's spec: its tracker item, mirrored, and the sections later steps added: \`.agents/specs/lt-1/spec.md\`
- \`handoff\` — this unit's handoff, which you keep current: \`.agents/specs/lt-1/handoffs/lt-1.md\`
- \`last_check\` — the last check's result. no check has run for this unit yet

norn's files in this worktree are under \`.agents/\` and ignored by git: the story's spec and this unit's handoff in \`.agents/specs/<story>/\`, the other inputs in \`.agents/inputs/\`. Edit the handoff, and add your sections to the spec below its item block; norn reads both back when you report, and the slice files in its \`slices/\` folder too. The other inputs are rewritten for every run.

## Handoff

\`.agents/specs/lt-1/handoffs/lt-1.md\` is this unit's current state, for the next agent and the person. Rewrite it in place, never as a diary, and refresh it right before you report.

## Rules

- Work only in this worktree and only on this step: nothing that belongs to later work, no drive-by changes.
- Never \`git push\`, never switch branches or worktrees, and never change the remote (a pull request, an issue, a comment): only norn does, under a receipt.
- Never edit norn's run state (its data folder outside the worktree): a hand-edited file fails validation and makes the unit corrupt.
- Of the norn commands, run only the four this brief names; every other one is the person's.
- A subagent you start works under these rules though it never sees this brief: tell it the ones its work can break.
- A usage limit is not a reason to report stuck: it resets on its own, and the session continues after it.

## Finish with exactly one of

- \`norn done done\`
- \`norn ask "<one self-contained question>"\` — you need a person's decision; the step waits until it is answered.
- \`norn stuck "<reason>"\` — you cannot go on; say what would unblock it.

Report exactly once, from the worktree root, with the outcome whose meaning is true. A refused report says what to do instead: do exactly that, then report again. After a report succeeds, end your turn.

After a long session or a lost thread, run \`norn brief\` again: it is rendered from the unit's record each time.
"
`;

